 turnover rises from a ~0.53\u20130.59 baseline to a saturated **1.0** across t\u224839396\u201340935, then decays back. A single sharp peak, not a flat elevated plateau \u2014 the signature of one transition, not gradual drift.\n2. **Wholesale partner replacement (step_03, step_04):** Comparing \u00b15000 halves around 39396: **n_in_a=138, n_in_b=97, n_removed=138, n_added=97, Jaccard=0.0**. Zero surviving edges. All 12 group members move together onto a *shared* new partner block ({156, 324, 617, 728, 756, 874, 1312, 1422, 1538, 1656, 1893}) having jointly used a shared old block ({57, 234, 235, 354, 535, 559, 641, 931, 1182, 1357, 1416, 1436, 1838}) \u2014 confirmed at member level for 103 (step_09) and 1826 (step_10).\n3. **Abruptness (step_12):** group activity 38\u219221\u21925\u21921 across consecutive 1000-unit bins spanning the change point; the handover is compressed, not smeared.\n\n**Confounders ruled out:**\n- *Volume artefact:* group events **fall** 188\u2192119 (step_05) and unique edges dip (step_02) while the background graph *grows* 832\u21921222 events (step_07, step_08). The switch is not driven by an activity surge.\n- *Node arrival/departure:* old partners remain active after the change point (step_13: 57, 234, 354, 1182 all still transacting) and new partners were already alive before it (step_14). Partners were available in both eras \u2014 the group *chose* different ones.\n- *Static connectivity insufficient:* ignoring time, 103 links to both 57 and 324 with equal weight (step_15, step_16); only the temporal split separates them.\n- *Background rewiring:* the graph-wide edge Jaccard is also ~0 (step_06), so that metric alone is uninformative here \u2014 the discriminating fact is the group's *coordinated* switch onto a common partner set, against a background of one-off edges (repeat_ratio 0.02\u20130.06).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_cd75e8ee\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [103, 462, 719, 836, 866, 1224, 1318, 1374, 1415, 1598, 1610, 1826],\n  \"window\": [34396, 44396],\n  \"confidence\": 0.86,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"At t~39396 all 12 reviewed nodes simultaneously abandon a shared partner block and adopt a disjoint new one: Jaccard 0.0 across the +/-5000 halves, with turnover spiking to 1.0 and returning to baseline afterwards. The switch is not explained by volume (group activity falls while the graph grows), nor by partner churn (old partners stay active afterwards, new partners existed beforehand), nor by static connectivity (time-ignored, old and new partners look identical).\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 59328, "total_output_tokens": 5137, "total_tokens": 64465, "total_latency_ms": 74786, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}